%n sebet | melemm[ū | ...]
#note: See @bib{RN127@38}: “The key phrase is @i{sebet melemmū} in l. 4′, which obviously alludes to Ḫuwawa’s supernatural protection, the seven cloaks bestowed on him by Enlil. In other versions of the poem these are referred to by three different expressions: (a) @i{sebe pulḫiʾātim} “seven terrors” (OB Gilg III 137) // @i{pulḫête} (Assyrian Gilg MS y obv. 15′) // @i{pulḫâti} (SB Gilg II 219b etc.); (b) @i{melemmū}, seven in number and likened to Ḫuwawa’s chicks (OB Gilg Ishchali 12′–13′); and (c) @i{namrirrū ša ilim} “the god’s radiant auras” (OB Gilg Nippur 3) in Enkidu’s explanation of Gilgameš’s third dream. The last is the context closest to the present one but there is no other similarity in the phrasing of the two passages.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "III" 137
// cf. L I.4 OB "Ishchali" 12'
// cf. L I.4 OB "Ishchali" 13'
// cf. L I.4 OB "Nippur" 3
// cf. L I.4 NA "y" a+15'b
// cf. L I.4 SB "II" 219a
// cf. L I.4 SB "II" 228
// cf. L I.4 SB "II" 285
// cf. L I.4 SB "II" 299

%n u ša t[āmurū(šu) | ...]
#note: See @bib{RN127@39}: “The phrase @i{ša tāmuru} is standard in the dream explanations of Gilgameš. It accompanies the identification of some figure in the dream with either the sun god Šamaš (OB Gilg Schøyen₂ = Text No. 5: 21: @i{u ša tāmurūšu}, OB Gilg Nippur rev. 6′, OB Gilg Harmal₁ 12), or Gilgameš’s father, Lugalbanda (OB Gilg Nippur 7), or an unidentified person (SB Gilg IV Y3 v 3′, broken).”
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 21
// cf. L I.4 OB "Nippur" 7
// cf. L I.4 OB "Nippur" a+6
// cf. L I.4 OB "Harmal₁" 12
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 30
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" b+3'

